# First-party SHACL Rules case: a CONSTANT literal subject makes every triple the
# rule would infer ill-formed, so the rule infers nothing and is not an error.
# SHACL 1.2 Inference Rules, "Execution of triple rules": "For each combination of
# members s of S, p of P and o of O, infer a triple with subject s, predicate p and
# object o. Skip ill-formed triples, for example when a blank node is used as
# predicate." A literal is a constant term node expression, so `sh:subject
# "notasubject"` is a well-formed rule whose one combination is skipped. The
# control rule differs only in its IRI subject, and its triple is inferred.

@prefix ex:  <http://example.org/ns#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix sh:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .

ex:alice rdf:type ex:Person .

ex:LiteralSubjectRule
  rdf:type sh:NodeShape ;
  sh:targetClass ex:Person ;
  sh:rule [
      rdf:type sh:TripleRule ;
      sh:subject "notasubject" ;
      sh:predicate ex:p ;
      sh:object ex:o ] ;
  sh:rule [
      rdf:type sh:TripleRule ;
      sh:subject ex:alice ;
      sh:predicate ex:q ;
      sh:object ex:o ] .
